<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Atoni</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Atoin Meto, Dawan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Atoni, or Atoin Meto, are the largest people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-timor">West Timor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, speaking the Uab Meto language. Traditionally they lived in dispersed hamlets in the mountains, growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, and were organised in domains under rulers with ritual and political roles, such as Sonbai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanuban">Amanuban</a> and Amarasi. Most converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atoni.jpg" alt="Atoni" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COLLECTIE_TROPENMUSEUM_De_meo_voorvechter_en_priester_van_Oekabiti_in_oorlogskostuum_met_andere_atoni%27s_TMnr_10005969.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
